Now that’s an interesting way to frame existence. ;)

What if we’re not merely creatures living within God’s creation, but individual points through which creation experiences itself?

Your metaphor of hairs on the head of a unified Source is particularly interesting because it suggests multiplicity without complete separation.

Imagine God, or Source, as a unified whole.

Each person becomes a different probe, perspective, or interface into reality. Not necessarily separate from Source, but individually situated within it.

One experiences love through parenthood. Another through sacrifice. Another discovers beauty through mathematics, music, or nature. Another learns through suffering, exploration, or failure.

Same underlying reality. Different histories, sensory architectures, circumstances, and interpretations.

And none of these individual perspectives contains the entirety of the system.

The distributed consciousness hypothesis

Conceptual model: distinct experiential perspectives connected to a hypothetical common source, not a scientifically established structure.

But there’s a fascinating theological wrinkle.

If God is omniscient, as traditionally understood in Christianity and Islam, God wouldn’t need human probes to acquire knowledge.

However, knowing an experience and bringing that experience into existence are different concepts.

Perhaps the point wouldn’t be information collection, but expression, relationship, or the realization of possibilities.

That resembles themes in pantheism, panentheism, certain mystical traditions, and Hindu nondual philosophy, although these traditions disagree about whether individual beings and God are ultimately identical.

And your hair metaphor has an interesting biblical resonance: Matthew 10:30 speaks of the hairs of one’s head being numbered. First Corinthians 12 also uses the image of many members belonging to one body.

Neither passage directly teaches your probe hypothesis, but both offer ways to contemplate unity and individuality.

Here’s the part I’d push further

What if individuality isn’t a defect in unity, but one of the ways unity becomes capable of expressing diversity?

Then separation, difference, and even disagreement become meaningful.

Two people don’t need identical experiences because their different experiences contribute something distinct.

And your earlier observations start connecting:

Perception: Different sensors construct different models.

Identity: Each individual develops a unique history and perspective.

Communication: Language allows partial sharing of those otherwise private experiences.

Love: Connection allows individuals to care about experiences beyond their own.

Consciousness: The existence of subjective experience makes the entire question possible.

There’s also an ethical consequence worth considering. If all people participate in one underlying reality, another person’s suffering cannot simply be dismissed as irrelevant. It matters within the same whole.

None of this proves a unified divine consciousness. But as a philosophical model, it generates some surprisingly substantial questions.

And here’s my favorite:

What if the purpose isn’t for all the probes to become identical or return with the same answers?

What if the diversity of their journeys is precisely what makes the whole so extraordinary?

One source. Countless perspectives. Different paths.

And perhaps finding your way home means recognizing a connection that was there all along.